  {
    "episode_id": 79349,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/9/79349.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Well, I had written a previous book called Angry Child to deal with children and adolescents and quite frankly how anger, childhood anger goes into adulthood. What I also found in this and perhaps a lot of it because of folks that I work with are are so involved with um... anger that they end up uh... using techniques of anger that uh... can be hidden and that is you know it always makes the evening news when someone you know smashes someone's car or punches them are shootings all that said big aggressive behavior but what comes out when um... with hidden anger is that it's the sneaky things. It's holding back. It's covert action. It's not doing all you could. It's the false compliment. It's got something behind someone's back. It's all those passive things you do that does not directly deal with someone. And quite frankly, it is kind of the theme of our time on social media There's a lot of direct things that take place, you know, attacks, insults, obnoxious statements. But don't you know, it's not like someone saying, my name is John Doe and I disagree with you and here's what it is. They always use phony names. They will be snarky and sarcastic. oftentimes deriding people, but it's not the direct kind of communication that really can work towards a solution. Our whole society seems to be going that way. And I know my day job in Congress, well, quite frankly, there's a lot of this passive aggression there. And I think in any workplace, people see it more and more in their workplace, in their families, in their marriages or past marriages. And it's something that I see is so pervasive in society, I had to write a book on it because I didn't see a guide that could really help us out of this.",
