  {
    "episode_id": 79349,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/9/79349.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Well, a lot of it comes from just the way people learned to face conflict, not handle it, but face it. So from childhood, that whether anger for them was a voice of pain or a voice of indulgence or the voice of just attack, that they learned to use anger to respond to problems. But what happened is they didn't learn a more effective technique. I mean, let's face it, as a communication takes work, to be able to sit down, face to face with someone with whom you're having a conflict and say, hey, we need to talk. Neither one of us is benefiting from this problem. Let's face it. Let's come up with a solution. Let's tell me your ideas. Mine. Let's work this out. That takes time. But anger and rage and the issues of the just the festering wounds take a lot more time, a lot more energy and can be emotionally and quite frankly, physically and medically damaging. But for some people, they just didn't learn another way. And so they think that, hey, I'm going to get someone back. And then we, we reward it. I mean, how many times do we hear stories of people that say, you know, I was in the grocery store and I'm standing in line waiting for something. The person in front of me stuck out a checkbook and there's all this. I took wherever, never. So I showed her, I took one of her items and I took it out of her basket and she'll wonder where was her catching when she gets home. You know, okay, well, big deal. What did you prove there? Or, you know, the thing that people do in parking lots, I talked too close to him so he couldn't get out of his car. Whatever it is. And then we say, yeah, you go, you go, you get him, you get him. Big deal. But we use this as a way of somehow patting ourselves in the fact that, look what I did, I hurt somebody. It doesn't work. It just makes things worse. And so whether you're doing the workplace to stick it to another employee or stick it to the boss or stick it to employees or in your marriage, or if you're using your marriage a lot, you're soon to be ex-marriage. It is just something that's kind of, we tell ourselves it's self-rewarding because we think that denting is actually healthy. I mean, how we time to hear people say, you know, why don't you just let it out? Go ahead and express your emotions. Punch this pillow, throw something, smash this glass, and you'll feel better. Well, you may feel better for a second if that's your self-rewarding, say, your way of doing things, but it sure isn't gonna work and you're actually gonna end up feeling worse in the long run.",
