    "turn_text": "well let's read it down in a few steps here because whether you're working in a factory, in a medical department, a hospital, and a lab, anywhere, you're going to see these behaviors creep up. Now, luckily, in most cases, people, you can write it off at just a bad day. Someone's grumpy, someone is out of sorts, and you can pick that up. And if someone's not getting the work done or the thing, sometimes just calling attention to it is simple phrase like, are you okay? Something's different today. Is there anything you need? That can sometimes just shine a little light on the person, help them understand that you care about them. And maybe sometimes even hear something that could be, you know, my daughter's really sick. I got terrible financial problems or things. Or sometimes people say, I'll be okay, just a bad day, leave me alone. I'll say, okay, well, that's fine. Then redirect them to see her. Let's get back on target, back on task. The concern comes when you're dealing with someone who handles this quantically. quite frankly, just a pain in the neck to work with, because they shun responsibility. They set things up so that the errors are blamed on you. They make a toxic environment in this. And we have to be able to discern, is it them or is it me who does it? This could be the arrest of a boss, the disagreeable student, the backstabbing coke worker. And these things really make people want to leave that office space. So here's a few things. identify who it is. I mean, are you kind of person who feels bothered when requests are made of you? Do you keep your feelings to yourself? Are you someone who gets upset by criticism? Do you like to appear successful in everything? How do you handle frustration and anger? And the reason I'm saying these things in which we have a further checklist in the book is just Get a sense of your own self and how you handle conflict because sometimes people are responding to your own inability to communicate directly or your own grumpiness and style. And sometimes it helps to just keep your own personal and journal. What gets you angry? What are the stressors on the job? And even if you see that it is one person and what they do, just writing down what happened sometimes helped. So here's something that I knew, someone who, a woman who didn't like her boss very much. And so what she did to her boss was, well, just got, it was, had to book seats on an airplane, kind of boss the seats that, well, were way in the back, why the restroom, seats that didn't go down the middle, just gave a knowing seat. Oh, I'm sorry, that was the only seats left. or double book appointments or made it so that her boss couldn't get from one point to another in time. Now, that eventually became a situation where the boss came to the employee and said, look, this is not working out. Let's talk factually through this. And after two or three attempts to get things right, It appeared that either this employee wasn't going to get it, or was doing it with on purpose. And eventually that boss had to say to employee, look, we're going to transfer you, get you out of the job. Then the employee backs it up by telling other employees, isn't the boss terrible, look at her boss, she's terrible, he's awful, she's mean, she's picking on me. That's why it's important when you're in a situation like this, write it down and document. If it continues on, document it because if that person deals with others in a passive aggressive behavior, they also have the potential to be very aggressive in that. So be careful. Another thing is you sometimes are dealing with people who are just are burned out. They feel a compassion fatigue. You see this a lot in hospitals where people are so used to pouring their heart out, helping people out who are sick, who are struggling, that they themselves are not taking care of themselves. It's that candle that is burnt out and it is important when you see that for the people, sometimes it's just good to say, hey, you need a break. Go take, you need a vacation. You need to take time off because you're getting grumpy here. You're missing things and that's a problem. And the, the we though is just kind of assess yourself. Now with some of the, Anger types you may see at work or school are, be trained someone with secrets, having other people take credit for your work, breaking competences, telling gossip, maybe plays up a certain person with authority and treats the boardness poorly, maybe sabotages other works. And so the way you handle those kind of things is approach people with kindness. Don't gossip. Do want to others as you would have them doing to you. Make sure you're documenting your own successes. We also have a person, that first person we call the backstabber, the next person we call the Avenger. And this is when they lash out. They don't really have options. They don't know how to solve problems. So they'll obstruct your goals, they'll sabotage your work, they'll pull the wool over other people's eyes. They'll criticize you behind your back. And those are ones where being respectful of others is helpful. Don't let other people blame you for what's not your fault to speak up yourself, but don't do revenge yourself. And one other kind of personal workplace I want to mention is, well, for lack of better term, we call them the EOR. Now, remember the Winnie the Pooh story about EOR? Yes. Voice plan, I'm terrible. Nothing's going to work. So this is the kind of person who uses passive aggressive behavior by calling insults themselves. I know if one person was talking to co-workers, you know, you keep making the same mistake over again. And this is causing a problem for this person, as I know you're mad at me because I'm fat. Well, sure, call me despicable. I know you all hate me. I said, whoa, I didn't say that at all. I just said you kept making the same mistake. Yeah, I hear what you're saying. So it is this ear mentality that I'm the victim. Nothing's going to work. The glasses have empty. I feel sorry for myself. And the way to handle that is be direct. Be focused. Ignore those victim kind of statements and correct them. I didn't say that all. You know, you're a good worker. So you make it very clear. But don't accept their redirection into the insult. Put it back into facts. Be careful getting into emotional statements with that. And sometimes just hand them back to work until this isn't done. You got to do it again. Because sometimes this person who wants you to feel sorry for them will intentionally set up a failure, blame you for that or set up their own failure. So you come back and chew them out and say, see, nobody likes me. You don't appreciate my work. And that's the way you undermine that you're a kind of person. I mean, there's several types of persons there in the workplace, but those are some of them and how you can respond back.",
