    "turn_text": "Well, even when you are in a loving relation, a class of aggression can rear its ugly head in the tiniest ways. One of the ways is the detached person who just isn't emotionally available at times. Now, this is different from, Hey, listen, I just had a bad day. Can I have some space on my own? That's okay. That is a way that a person in that relationship, whether they're spouse or love, whatever can say, okay, I'll be supportive, sure. But that time when someone says, hey, can I just have some alone time to kind of get things back together, can also be undermined by the press of aggressive person saying, okay, fine, you want to be alone? Do you want to ignore me? Fine. And then while this person's saying, I just need quiet time in the living, I'm just to debrief my own head here. That person's banging the pots and pans, running the vacuum cleaner, making noise, running the dog through doing some things to bug the person asked not to do. But when it goes beyond just that, when it is really a detached relationship, it's no longer sharing the feelings, no longer talking about those special moments, no longer saying, Hey, I just want to bounce an idea. If you have, I'm struggling with this thought in my own head, the solid relationship that builds that emotional intimacy. We'll openly talk about those things and try to communicate. Now, granted, women are much better at some of the things about communicating with men. Yeah, different brains than we do. You're paying more attention to emotional data than men do. That's just a fact. And we can really accept that. But when someone intentionally withholds information, I'm not going to talk about it because I don't want to share this with you because I just want to annoy you and bug you and be detached. That's when it gets into a problem. It could also be, it's a guy named John Gottman, who's one of the Gottman Institute of Psychologists, talks about things like criticism and contempt and inventiveness and stonewalling. All can predict that something is going to go over 90% of the time when someone's going to divorce if they're all in there. It could be a certain selflessness. It could be the little nitpicking criticism. It could be withholding compliments or all part of what can be very, very damaging in relationship. And then from that, you also have things like People who are negativistic, people who are dealing with the financial distresses of a home, and someone is not doing the right things to save the money to be a little more responsible with that, are all parts that feed into these relationships. And boy, they can be so toxic, they can just destroy something that was once highly romantic love and finally just chip away and chip away until it just dies.",
